{"subjectId":"java","id":"primitive-types","title":"Primitive Types & Variables","group":"Getting Started","order":5,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Java has exactly <strong>8 primitive types</strong> with fixed sizes on every platform; everything else is a <em>reference</em> to an object.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>The 8 primitives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Size</th><th>Range / values</th><th>Default</th><th>Literal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>byte</code></td><td>8 bit</td><td>−128 … 127</td><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>(byte) 10</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>short</code></td><td>16 bit</td><td>−32,768 … 32,767</td><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>(short) 10</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int</code></td><td>32 bit</td><td>−2³¹ … 2³¹−1 (≈ ±2.1 billion)</td><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>42</code>, <code>0x2A</code>, <code>0b101010</code>, <code>1_000_000</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>long</code></td><td>64 bit</td><td>−2⁶³ … 2⁶³−1 (≈ ±9.2 × 10¹⁸)</td><td><code>0L</code></td><td><code>42L</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>float</code></td><td>32 bit</td><td>IEEE 754, ~7 significant digits</td><td><code>0.0f</code></td><td><code>3.14f</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>double</code></td><td>64 bit</td><td>IEEE 754, ~15–16 significant digits</td><td><code>0.0d</code></td><td><code>3.14</code>, <code>1e-9</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>char</code></td><td>16 bit</td><td>UTF-16 code unit <code>'\\u0000'</code> … <code>'\\uffff'</code> (unsigned)</td><td><code>'\\u0000'</code></td><td><code>'A'</code>, <code>'\\n'</code>, <code>'\\u0041'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>JVM-dependent</td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code> (not 0/1!)</td><td><code>false</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(Byte.MIN_VALUE + \" \" + Byte.MAX_VALUE);\n        System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE + \" \" + Long.MAX_VALUE);\n        System.out.println((int) Character.MAX_VALUE + \" \" + Character.SIZE + \" bits\");\n        int million = 1_000_000;                   // underscores for readability\n        System.out.println(million + \" \" + 0xFF + \" \" + 0b1010 + \" \" + 017);\n        char c = 'A';\n        System.out.println(c + 1);                 // char promoted to int\n        System.out.println((char) (c + 1));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>-128 127\n2147483647 9223372036854775807\n65535 16 bits\n1000000 255 10 15\n66\nB</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A leading <code>0</code> makes an <em>octal</em> literal: <code>017</code> is 15, and <code>09</code> doesn't compile.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-variable-plus\"></i>Variables: local, instance, static</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Declared</th><th>Lives in</th><th>Default value?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Local</td><td>Inside a method/block</td><td>Stack frame</td><td><strong>No</strong> — must be assigned before use (compile error otherwise)</td></tr><tr><td>Instance (field)</td><td>In the class, no <code>static</code></td><td>Inside each object on the heap</td><td>Yes (<code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Static (class variable)</td><td><code>static</code> in the class</td><td>Once per class</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int x;\n        System.out.println(x);   // local variable never assigned\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Compiler error</figcaption><pre><code>Main.java:4: error: variable x might not have been initialized\n        System.out.println(x);   // local variable never assigned\n                           ^\n1 error</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i><code>var</code> — local type inference (Java 10+)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var count = 10;                       // int\n        var price = 9.99;                     // double\n        var names = new ArrayList&lt;String&gt;();  // ArrayList&lt;String&gt;\n        names.add(\"Ada\");\n        for (var n : names) System.out.println(n + \" \" + count + \" \" + price);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Ada 10 9.99</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>Still <strong>static typing</strong> — the compiler infers the type once; it can't change later.</li><li>Only for local variables with an initialiser (and loop variables). Not for fields, parameters or return types.</li><li><code>var x = null;</code> and <code>var a = {1, 2};</code> don't compile — there's nothing to infer from.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Integer overflow &amp; floating-point surprises</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int big = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        System.out.println(big + 1);                   // wraps around silently\n        long ok = big + 1L;                            // do the math in long\n        System.out.println(ok);\n        System.out.println(1_000_000 * 1_000_000);     // int * int overflows\n        System.out.println(1_000_000L * 1_000_000);\n        try {\n            Math.addExact(big, 1);\n        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"addExact: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n        System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);\n        System.out.println(0.1f + 0.2f);\n        System.out.println(1.0 / 0 + \" \" + -1.0 / 0 + \" \" + 0.0 / 0);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>-2147483648\n2147483648\n-727379968\n1000000000000\naddExact: integer overflow\n0.30000000000000004\n0.3\nInfinity -Infinity NaN</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Integer overflow is <strong>silent</strong> in Java. Use <code>long</code>, <code>Math.addExact</code>/<code>multiplyExact</code> (which throw), or <code>BigInteger</code>. For money, never use <code>double</code> — use <code>BigDecimal</code> or integer cents.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.math.BigDecimal;\nimport java.math.BigInteger;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(new BigDecimal(\"0.1\").add(new BigDecimal(\"0.2\")));\n        System.out.println(new BigDecimal(0.1));    // the double's exact binary value!\n        BigInteger f = BigInteger.ONE;\n        for (int i = 2; i &lt;= 25; i++) f = f.multiply(BigInteger.valueOf(i));\n        System.out.println(\"25! = \" + f);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0.3\n0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625\n25! = 15511210043330985984000000</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is char 16 bits in Java?</summary><div><p>Java was designed around Unicode, and in 1995 Unicode fit in 16 bits. A <code>char</code> is a UTF-16 <em>code unit</em>; characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane (e.g. many emoji) need two <code>char</code>s (a surrogate pair).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are default values, and do local variables get them?</summary><div><p>Fields and array elements get defaults (<code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>'\\u0000'</code>, <code>null</code>). Local variables do <strong>not</strong> — the compiler rejects reading one before it's definitely assigned.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does var make Java dynamically typed?</summary><div><p>No. <code>var</code> is compile-time inference only; the variable has one fixed static type, exactly as if you had written it out.</p></div></details></section>"}